{"builder":"Criteria the build must meet (from the issue, word for word):\n- index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\n- padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\n- README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nSteps:\n1. Read index.js, README.md, package.json and test/strutil.test.js. index.js currently exports capitalize, slugify and truncate; README.md has sections only for capitalize and truncate, so slugify is already undocumented.\n2. In index.js add and export `padLeft(s, width, fill = ' ')`, following the style of the existing functions (short doc comment, ES module export). It returns s with fill prepended until the result is exactly width characters (for example `s.padStart(width, fill)`), and returns s unchanged when s.length >= width. Example: padLeft('42', 5, '0') === '00042'; padLeft('42', 5) === '   42'; padLeft('12345', 3, '0') === '12345'.\n3. In README.md add a `## padLeft(s, width, fill)` section describing the behaviour, including the default space fill and the unchanged-when-long-enough rule, and add a `## slugify(s)` section (lower case, words joined by dashes, leading/trailing dashes removed), so that every function exported by index.js has its own `## name(...)` section. Keep the existing sections and the Tests section.\n4. In test/strutil.test.js import padLeft and add tests covering: zero padding ('42', 5, '0' -> '00042'), default space fill, and returning s unchanged when its length equals width and when it exceeds width.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure all tests pass. Then open one pull request with these changes.","verifier":"Check out the PR head, run `npm install` if a lockfile or dependencies were added (none are expected), and run each check below in order. Report PASS or FAIL for each with the command output.\n\nCheck 1 — expect: \"index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\"\nRun: `node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; const p=m.padLeft; console.log(typeof p, JSON.stringify([p('42',5,'0'), p('42',5), p('a',4,'x'), p('',3,'0')]))\"`\nPASS: prints `function [\"00042\",\"   42\",\"xxxa\",\"000\"]`.\nFAIL: padLeft is not exported as a function, the command errors, or any value differs (padding on the wrong side, wrong length, or a fill other than space when fill is omitted).\n\nCheck 2 — expect: \"padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\"\nRun: `node --input-type=module -e \"import {padLeft as p} from './index.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify([p('12345',5,'0'), p('123456',5,'0'), p('abc',2), p('abc',0,'*')]))\"`\nPASS: prints `[\"12345\",\"123456\",\"abc\",\"abc\"]`.\nFAIL: the command errors or any result differs from its input s (e.g. truncated or padded).\n\nCheck 3 — expect: \"README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\"\nRun: `node --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; import fs from 'node:fs'; const r=fs.readFileSync('README.md','utf8'); const fns=Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function'); const missing=fns.filter(f=>!new RegExp('^#{1,6}\\\\s+\\x60?'+f+'\\\\b','m').test(r)); console.log('exports:',fns.join(','),'missing:',missing.join(',')||'none')\"`\nAlso open README.md and confirm each heading found is a section about that function.\nPASS: the output reports `missing: none`, and the exports list includes capitalize, slugify, truncate and padLeft (plus any others), each with its own README heading section.\nFAIL: any exported function (note: slugify had no section before this change) lacks a README heading section.\n\nCheck 4 — expect: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: `npm test`\nPASS: exit code 0 with no failing tests reported.\nFAIL: non-zero exit code or any failing test.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- Check that the new tests in test/strutil.test.js actually exercise padLeft (default fill, explicit fill, s at and above width).\n- Note whether padLeft with a multi-character fill behaves sensibly (e.g. result length exactly width), and whether the README sections describe behaviour accurately."}